  1779	    We inspect ONLY the `gpgsig` header, NOT the whole object: a commit's MESSAGE
  1780	    is attacker-controlled, so a GPG-signed commit whose message merely contains
  1781	    the text 'BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE' must not be mistaken for ssh-signed (Sol R4 —
  1782	    a spoof of the substring check). Commit headers end at the first blank line;
  1783	    the signature is the `gpgsig` header (its base64 continues on space-indented
  1784	    lines), so we read only up to that blank line and only the gpgsig header."""
  1785	    obj = git(["cat-file", "commit", sha], cwd=root)
  1786	    if not obj:
  1787	        return False
  1788	    header = obj.partition("\n\n")[0]  # headers only — drop the message
  1789	    for line in header.split("\n"):
  1790	        if line.startswith("gpgsig "):
  1791	            return "SSH SIGNATURE" in line  # 'gpgsig -----BEGIN SSH SIGNATURE-----'
  1792	    return False
