  2205	# The words real reviewers actually end on. Sampled from the fleet's captured
  2206	# transcripts: Codex ends "VERDICT: PASS", GLM's red-team scale is
  2207	# "Verdict: PASS / PASS WITH RISKS / REQUEST CHANGES / BLOCK", and some runs
  2208	# emit lowercase "verdict: fail" or a GitHub-style "APPROVE". A parser that
  2209	# only knew uppercase "VERDICT: PASS" scored every one of those a fail —
  2210	# fail-closed, but useless: it would have made `check` unusable with the very
  2211	# reviewers this repo ships. Unknown wording still fails.
  2212	# The reviewer's verdict is a MACHINE PROTOCOL, not prose to be interpreted.
  2213	# Exactly one canonical marker line counts:
  2214	#
  2215	#     VERDICT: PASS        (or)        VERDICT: FAIL
  2216	#
  2217	# The reviewer PROMPT tells the reviewer to end with it; the gate does not try
  2218	# to infer approval from natural language. "PASS WITH RISKS", "APPROVE",
  2219	# "LGTM", "SHIP" and every other human phrasing are NOT a pass — inferring one
  2220	# would let a hedged review land as `pass` with zero open findings. Missing,
  2221	# malformed, truncated, ambiguous or non-canonical: FAIL CLOSED.
